## Third-party content

This project packages, and its built container images bake in, content from
three upstream projects. They do NOT share an upstream or a license. Two
are OWASP Foundation projects licensed CC-BY-SA-4.0, verified via the GitHub
licence API on each upstream repository; the third is MITRE's, under MITRE's
own terms of use:

- OWASP Application Security Verification Standard (ASVS)
  https://github.com/OWASP/ASVS
  License: CC-BY-SA-4.0 (see LICENSE-OWASP-ASVS for the full text)

- OWASP Cheat Sheet Series
  https://github.com/OWASP/CheatSheetSeries
  License: CC-BY-SA-4.0 (see LICENSE-OWASP-CHEATSHEET for the full text)

- Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE) — The MITRE Corporation (not OWASP)
  https://cwe.mitre.org/
  License: LicenseRef-CWE-TOU (see LICENSE-CWE for the full terms)

Baking this content into a distributable image is redistribution, and the
SQLite index built from it — chunked, cross-linked, and re-served through the
MCP tools — is an adaptation under the Creative Commons framework.

The index is a COMPOSITE of differently-licensed content, and no single
license covers all of it. The portions derived from ASVS and the Cheat Sheet
Series remain CC-BY-SA-4.0 in their own right as an adaptation; each carries
its own copy of that license, LICENSE-OWASP-ASVS and LICENSE-OWASP-CHEATSHEET.
The two files hold the same text because the two projects chose the same
license, not because they are one work: they are pinned separately and either
could be relicensed without the other. The portion derived from the CWE
catalogue is governed by MITRE's CWE Terms of Use; see LICENSE-CWE for that
text, which must accompany redistribution. Each image's OCI labels state
the same split (`org.opencontainers.image.licenses="MIT AND CC-BY-SA-4.0 AND
LicenseRef-CWE-TOU"`), and docs/licensing.md gives the practical reading —
neither that document nor this one is legal advice.

The MIT license in LICENSE covers only the project's own source code (the
ingest pipeline, the MCP server, the plugin and skills), never the upstream
content or the index derived from it.

For the exact pinned version, release tag or commit SHA of each upstream
source in any given build, see `sources.lock.yml`, or call the `kb_info` MCP
tool against a running server — it reports the loaded pack versions directly
from the index that was actually built, which is the authoritative source
once an image has shipped.
